f="/metric-admission/"><span>19</span>Source metrics</a><a href="/subdivision/"><span>20</span>Subdivision</a><a href="/metric-selection/"><span>21</span>Metric selection</a><a href="/refinement-tower/"><span>22</span>Refinement tower</a><a href="/history-comparison/"><span>23</span>History comparison</a><a href="/observation/"><span>24</span>Observation</a></nav><div class="sidebar-bottom"><span class="status-dot"></span> Physical promotion <b>0</b><p>Independent human review<br>pending</p><a href="https://github.com/ud-tetra/UD">Research repository ↗</a></div></aside><div class="page-shell"><header class="topbar"><span>FINITE STRUCTURE / OPEN CORRESPONDENCE</span><a href="/sources/">Search the record <span>⌕</span></a></header><main id="main"><div class="page-intro"><div class="eyebrow">Updates and next work</div><h1>A methodical consolidation.</h1><p class="lede">Dated development notes live here. Current source versions, unresolved coverage and next gates stay visible.</p></div><article class="claim" id="preparation-certificate-v01"><div class="eyebrow">18 October 2026 UTC · Theorem milestone 0.1</div><h2>Preparation now has a gauge-invariant sufficient certificate</h2><p>A global orbit-distance upper score combines 27 real invariant inputs. Exact rational enclosures can establish entry into the previous endpoint trapping ball, with no prior local-chart assumption. Norm/Gauss validity, measurement coverage and model-error bounds remain separate inputs.</p><p>The eight-page theorem also proves an obstruction: configuration-only observations miss arbitrary admissible electric circulation, and active loop histories miss independent unused-color dynamics at rank ≥2. The exact calculator, 46 finite-topology/rational checks and numerical matrix controls are available. The conservative unit raw-interval allocation is about 2.93×10⁻⁴³ in model units, a sufficient bound rather than a physical precision requirement. Independent review pending; physical promotion 0.</p><a class="text-link" href="/papers/#preparation-certificate-v01">Read the preparation theorem →</a></article><article class="claim" id="observation-session-v01"><div class="eyebrow">17 October 2026 · Session publication</div><h2>600-cell observation, finite calibration and memory-limited spectra</h2><p>One 62-channel vertex link has ideal history rank 1893 and 747 hidden dimensions. A second link plus a tetrahedral port leaves 142; a separate 189-channel observer is complete for the declared nominal model. Finite conditioning and calibration certificates specify their own error assumptions.</p><p>The frozen 37-preparation record has 18,790,154 scalar readings. Verified Hankel actions, factor Grams and residual bounds avoid a 286.75-GiB dense allocation. Record-only and bounded-noise certificates preserve the distinction between retained threshold count and exact rank. All 26 accessible research packages/captures are now indexed in merged PR #179.</p><p>Independent review remains HOLD pending attributable, hash-bound corrected returns. Constructor verification is not experimental validation; physical promotion 0.</p><div class="actions"><a class="button secondary" href="/observation/">Read the session results →</a><a class="text-link" href="https://github.com/ud-tetra/UD/pull/179">Merged evidence ↗</a><a class="text-link" href="https://github.com/users/ud-tetra/projects/1/views/1?pane=info&amp;statusUpdateId=289526">Project status ↗</a></div></article><article class="claim" id="endpoint-robustness-v01"><div class="eyebrow">16 October 2026 UTC · Theorem milestone 0.1</div><h2>The quartic endpoint now has explicit model-error allowances</h2><p>A cubic gradient budget covers generic Hamiltonian forces; centered errors with zero derivative on the nominal orbit use a quadratic curvature budget. Their shared inequality gives all-time static trapping and a compact minimizing invariant set with cube-root/square-root displacement bounds. A separate cumulative gradient-variation requirement handles time-dependent drift.</p><p>The eight-page theorem, exact calculator and mathematical requirements contract translate kinetic, magnetic, common/residual hopping and detuning errors into one budget. Loop readout limits include an instrument reserve and explicitly do not certify full preparation distance. An admitted stiffness increase shows why finite-radius trapping does not imply retained stability of the exact critical orbit. Twenty-four exact algebraic checks, five rational budget examples and four floating matrix diagnostics pass; frozen PET support remains conditional. Development, no registry entry; independent review pending; physical promotion 0.</p><a class="text-link" href="/papers/#endpoint-robustness-v01">Read the endpoint robustness theorem →</a></article><article class="claim" id="endpoint-parity-v01"><div class="eyebrow">16 October 2026 UTC · Theorem milestone 0.1</div><h2>The endpoint radius improves by at least 50,000</h2><p>The critical background has an isometric energy reflection that reverses the three soft directions and fixes their leading fast-relaxation correction. Three intrinsic tensor cancellations replace the earlier fifth-order loss by an explicit sixth-order bound. An exact polynomial comparison proves the radius improvement for every positive kinetic coefficient and matter norm at each finite rank.</p><p>The quartic energy coercivity and all-time trapping theorem remain valid on the larger tube. The seven-page proof, exact calculator and replay package include 22 exact conditions, four rational budget examples and four floating matrix diagnostics. The earlier EQT certificate is retained as conditional support, not counted as new interval evidence. This is a larger sufficient mathematical guarantee; actual basins, endpoint forcing/model errors and laboratory tolerances remain open. Development, no registry entry; independent review pending; physical promotion 0.</p><a class="text-link" href="/papers/#endpoint-parity-v01">Read the parity-sharpened theorem →</a></article><article class="claim" id="endpoint-quartic-v01"><div class="eyebrow">16 October 2026 UTC · Theorem milestone 0.1</div><h2>The quartically stable endpoint now has an explicit radius</h2><p>The original critical orbit at ε=0 satisfies H−H*≥(N/1600)d⁴ in a specified tube. A boundary energy barrier Bη=Nη⁴/1600 and a smaller initial ball give all-time trapping, including the invariant zero-Gauss subset, at every finite rank. The proof converts the frozen relaxed quartic into the product metric and absorbs an explicit fifth-order remainder.</p><p>Two new 192-bit Arb positive congruences, the frozen exact integer quartic replay and four exact budget examples passed. The eight-page manuscript and calculator are available. At a=N=1, R*≈2.36008×10⁻¹⁴, B≈1.93905×10⁻⁵⁸ and δ≈2.78500×10⁻³⁰. These conservative mathematical bounds close the quantitative endpoint slot left open in G14/G15; no sharp basin, endpoint model-error budget or physical calibration is established. Development, no registry entry; independent review pending; physical promotion 0.</p><a class="text-link" href="/papers/#endpoint-quartic-v01">Read the quartic-endpoint theorem →</a></article><article class="claim" id="robust-trapping-v01"><div class="eyebrow">16 October 2026 UTC · Theorem milestone 0.1</div><h2>Nonlinear trapping now tolerates a declared model-error budget</h2><p>A sufficient static Hamiltonian-gradient budget gives all-time trapping around a selected nominal balanced orbit and a nearby compact minimizing invariant set. Time-dependent errors receive a cumulative variation condition, and a separate external-forcing contract limits injected work. An exact resonant oscillator shows why instantaneous smallness alone cannot support a general all-time guarantee.</p><p>The theorem translates kinetic, diamond, hopping and site-detuning errors into one gradient cap. It also bounds each complex loop readout error by 21η and supplies a split state/instrument noise reserve for real and oriented classifiers. All 30 new exact/symbolic checks and six synthetic matrix-gradient/readout fixtures passed; the prior interval certificate remains a dependency, not a new count. No experimental data or physical detector map is supplied. The nominal orbit need not remain invariant, and no unique displaced branch is proved. Development, no registry entry; independent review pending; physical promotion 0.</p><a class="text-link" href="/papers/#robust-trapping-v01">Read the model-robustness theorem →</a></article><article class="claim" id="energy-barrier-v01"><div class="eyebrow">16 October 2026 UTC · Theorem milestone 0.1</div><h2>A quantitative nonlinear trapping budget is certified</h2><p>The six balanced finite-offset phase branches now have an explicit outer trapping radius, boundary energy barrier and smaller sufficient initial ball at every finite rank. The proof certifies the slow scalar Hessian floor and bounds global second and third geodesic energy derivatives. It uses distance to the compact gauge/species-phase orbit and preserves the residual stabilizer constraint.</p><p>All 211 exact/rigorous interval checks passed across 64 new parameter cells. Six synthetic jet diagnostics passed; no experimental data are used. In a=N=1 at ε=10⁻⁵ the conservative bounds are R≈2.41935×10⁻⁷, B≈1.46332×10⁻¹⁹ and δ≈7.65067×10⁻¹¹ in the declared mathematical normalization. This closes G13’s explicit nonlinear-radius slot for positive offsets. The quartic endpoint needs a separate quantitative modulus; the bounds are not maximal basins or laboratory tolerances. Development, no registry entry; independent review pending; physical promotion 0.</p><a class="text-link" href="/papers/#energy-barrier-v01">Read the nonlinear energy-barrier theorem →</a></article><article class="claim" id="finite-rank-v01"><div class="eyebrow">16 October 2026 UTC · Theorem milestone 0.1</div><h2>The finite-offset full-rank stability gap is closed</h2><p>All six balanced phase-branch embeddings retain nonlinear orbital stability at every fixed finite rank across the certified interval 0&lt;ε≤10⁻⁵. A matter-aligned normal gauge cancels the mixed matter–color Hessian term exactly. A two-color determinant identity bounds the magnetic correction and leaves explicit positive color and vacuum frequency gaps. The twelve and eight scalar saddle embeddings remain unstable.</p><p>The constructor replay passed 28 exact/rigorous interval checks. Four floating full-matrix synthetic fixtures agree with the quadratic formula to within 8×10⁻⁸; these are numerical diagnostics, with no experimental samples. The stability neighborhood is local and is not given an explicit displacement radius. The coefficient interval is sufficient, not maximal; the residual global stabilizer moment-map condition is retained. This milestone supersedes the open full-rank finite-offset stability slot in G12. Development, no registry entry; independent review pending; physical promotion 0.</p><a class="text-link" href="/papers/#finite-rank-v01">Read the full-rank finite-offset theorem →</a></article><article class="claim" id="finite-offset-v01"><div class="eyebrow">15 October 2026 UTC · Theorem milestone 0.1</div><h2>Near-threshold existence now has a numerical validity region</h2><p>A regularized full stationary system certifies the 26 phase branches on 0&lt;ε≤10⁻⁵. The certificate covers the rescaled zero endpoint, all scalar Morse indices and explicit amplitude and loop-readout errors. It also gives sufficient deterministic noise budgets for both oriented phases and six labeled real loop amplitudes.</p><p>Exact symmetry sectors and scaled Schur complements bound the stable soft-mode frequency ratio to within 0.005 of its limiting value when a=N=r=1. The proof replay passed 1,536 nested interval boxes and 2,048 spectral cut tests. The interval is sufficient, not maximal; uniqueness is within the certified tubes. Higher-rank stationary embeddings are covered, while their full normal stability radius remains open. No experimental data are analyzed. Development, no registry entry; independent review pending; physical promotion 0.</p><a class="text-link" href="/papers/#finite-offset-v01">Read the finite-offset theorem →</a></article><article class="claim" id="branch-readout-v01"><div class="eyebrow">15 October 2026 UTC · Theorem milestone 0.1</div><h2>Branch observables and a bounded-error measurement design</h2><p>A matter-dressed Wilson observable is invariant under the full local U(r) gauge group. On the one-active-color branch embeddings its oriented phases recover the critical vector exactly, because the nonlinear fast correction cancels. Fourth-moment fingerprints distinguish the three families and a signed cubic separates the two body-diagonal permutation orbits.</p><p>The theorem proves a sufficient phase-noise margin and an exact information limit for real-only channels. A proposed measurement contract specifies phase, Gauss, calibration and small-response requirements. Two primary studies offer gauge or holonomy readout precedents; their different actions and geometries do not currently provide a direct UD branch test. This is a limited compatibility screen, with no empirical reanalysis. The frozen action and ledger are unchanged; independent review pending; physical promotion 0.</p><a class="text-link" href="/papers/#branch-readout-v01">Read the branch-readout theorem →</a></article><article class="claim" id="critical-bifurcation-v01"><div class="eyebrow">15 October 2026 UTC · Theorem milestone 0.1</div><h2>New stable phase equilibria appear beyond the curved-orbit threshold</h2><p>The exact stationary energy yields six balanced minima, twelve index-one saddles and eight index-two saddles for sufficiently small positive coefficient excess. These counts retain vertex labels and describe phase-amplitude patterns on the fixed carrier. The scalar local diagram is complete; the higher-rank theorem concerns embeddings of